ke it as parameter and return the new buffer (like it does currently). Actually the current code has `var fnArgsBuf []any` in Run. Since `executeLoop` may be restarted after a catch, and fnArgsBuf is used across calls, I'll pass a pointer or return it. Let me make executeLoop return the fnArgsBuf.\n\nNow, `handlePanic`:\n\n```go\nfunc (vm *VM) handlePanic(r any, program *Program) bool {\n    for i := len(vm.tryStack) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {\n        f := &vm.tryStack[i]\n        switch f.state {\n        case stateCatch, stateFinally:\n            // Errors in catch/finally propagate outward, but before unwinding\n            // we should run any finally blocks belonging to outer frames? \n            // Actually we are at frame i, and frames above i... wait, we search from top,\n            // so there are no frames above i that are active. Frames below are outer.\n            // Pop this frame and continue outward.\n            vm.tryStack = vm.tryStack[:i]\n            continue\n        case stateNormal:\n            if f.finallyIP >= 0 {\n                f.pendingError = r\n                f.state = stateFinally\n                vm.Stack = vm.Stack[:f.stackSize]\n                vm.ip = f.finallyIP\n                return true\n            }\n            if f.catchIP >= 0 {\n                f.state = stateCatch\n                vm.Stack = vm.Stack[:f.stackSize]\n                vm.push(runtime.NormalizePanic(r))\n                vm.ip = f.catchIP\n                return true\n            }\n            vm.tryStack = vm.tryStack[:i]\n            continue\n        }\n    }\n    return false\n}\n```\n\nWait, there's a bug: in the `stateCatch`/`stateFinally` cases, I pop frame i but there might be frames above i? No \u2014 since we iterate from top, at the point we examine frame i, all frames above i (indices > i) have already been examined and either handled (returned true) or popped. In the stateCatch/stateFinally case, we pop frame i and continue to i-1 (outer). But what about an inner frame above i that was in stateNormal and had a finally? If an error occurs inside an inner catch, the inner frame is in stateCatch (we're inside its catch body). We pop it. But the inner frame's finally \u2014 should it run? Yes, if the inner try had a finally, it should run when the inner catch throws. But by popping the inner frame without running its finally, we skip it. That's a semantic gap.\n\nLet me handle this: when an error occurs in a catch body (stateCatch) or finally body (stateFinally), and the frame has a finally that hasn't run yet, we should run that finally before propagating. For stateCatch: the frame's finally may not have run yet (it runs after catch body). So we should set state=finally, pendingError=r, jump to finallyIP. For stateFinally: the finally is already running; if it throws, we should override pending error and propagate outward \u2014 pop frame and continue.\n\nSo:\n```go\ncase stateCatch:\n    if f.finallyIP >= 0 {\n        f.pendingError = r\n        f.state = stateFinally\n        vm.Stack = vm.Stack[:f.stackSize]\n        vm.ip = f.finallyIP\n        return true\n    }\n    vm.tryStack = vm.tryStack[:i]\n    continue\ncase stateFinally:\n    vm.tryStack = vm.tryStack[:i]\n    continue\n```\n\nGood. This handles nested finallys: if inner catch throws, we jump to inner finally. If inner finally throws, we pop inner frame and search outer; outer frame (if stateNormal) will run its finally/catch. If outer is stateCatch, etc.\n\nNow, what about `OpEndTry` semantics:\n- When we reach OpEndTry normally (success or catch completed), the top frame should be in state normal (success path) or state catch (catch path) or state finally (uncaught error path with pendingError). \n- `OpEndTry`:\n  - Pop the top frame.\n  - If frame.pendingError != nil, re-panic with it.\n  - Otherwise continue.\nBut wait, on the uncaught error path, when we jump to finally, the finally body executes and then OpEndTry pops frame and re-panics with pendingError. Good.\n- On success path: try body executes, jumps to finally, finally executes, OpEndTry pops (no pending error). Good.\n- On catch path: catch body executes (state=catch), then falls into finally (state=finally), finally executes, OpEndTry pops (no pending error). Good.\n\nBut on success path, the frame state is still `normal` when OpEndTry is reached. On catch path, `OpFinally` sets state=finally before finally. Actually I said I'd emit `OpFinally` at the start of the finally block to set state=finally. Let me define:\n- `OpFinally`: sets top frame state = finally.\n- `OpCatch`: sets top frame state = catch.\n\nNow for the retry: `OpRetry`:\n```go\ncase OpRetry:\n    if len(vm.tryStack) == 0 {\n        panic(runtime.NewError(runtime.CustomError, \"retry is not allowed outside catch block\"))\n    }\n    f := &vm.tryStack[len(vm.tryStack)-1]\n    if f.retries >= 3 {\n        panic(runtime.NewError(runtime.RetryError, \"retry limit exceeded\"))\n    }\n    f.retries++\n    f.state = stateNormal\n    f.pendingError = nil\n    vm.Stack = vm.Stack[:f.stackSize]\n    vm.ip = f.tryIP\n```\n\nI need `tryIP` in the frame \u2014 the start of the try body. I'll store it in TryInfo.\n\nBut wait: if `retry` is inside a catch body, the top frame is that try's frame (state=catch). Good. If retry is used in a nested try's catch, top frame is the nested one. Good.\n\nWhat about retry used inside a finally? \"usable inside catch blocks\" \u2014 only in catch. In finally, top frame state is finally; I'll still allow retry? Spec says usable inside catch blocks; using outside raises error. Finally is not catch, so it should raise an error. I'll check `f.state == stateCatch`; if not, panic with \"retry is not allowed outside catch block\". Actually simpler: just allow it in catch; if state != catch, error.\n\nNow, what about `retry` used inside catch when there's no enclosing try (i.e., top frame is nil)? Error.\n\nNow the `OpThrow` opcode: currently `panic(vm.pop().