\nBut wait \u2014 the catch block is entered by handlePanic which sets ip=catchAddr. If there's no catch (e.g., only finally), catchIP=-1 and there's no catchAddr. Then handlePanic goes to finally path (if finally exists) or propagates.\n\nFor a try with only finally (no catch):\n```\nOpTry <info>       ; info has catchIP=-1, finallyIP=F\n<try body>\nOpJump toEnd\nF:\nOpFinally\n<finally body>\ntoEnd:\nOpEndTry\n```\nOn error in try body: handlePanic sees finallyIP >= 0, sets pendingError, state=finally, jumps to F. Finally runs, OpEndTry pops frame and re-panics. Good.\n\nFor a try with no catch and no finally \u2014 that's pointless (try { } without catch/finally). Does the parser allow `try {}`? Probably should allow but it's useless. I'll allow it; it just executes the body (with a frame that catches nothing and immediately re-panics... actually if no catch/finally, handlePanic pops frame and propagates, so behavior is same as without try). Fine.\n\nNow the TryInfo constant:\n```go\ntype TryInfo struct {\n    CatchIP   int\n    FinallyIP int\n    EndIP     int\n    TryIP     int\n}\n```\nBut these are bytecode addresses known at compile time. The compiler emits `OpTry` with arg = constant index of TryInfo. Since TryInfo is mutable? No, it's immutable after compile. Good.\n\nHowever, there's an issue: `OpTry` pushes a frame but the frame's stackSize must be set at runtime (current stack depth). That's fine.\n\nNow, `OpRetry` needs `tryIP` from the frame. I'll store TryIP in TryInfo.\n\nNow, the `OpCatch` opcode: emitted at the very start of the catch block? Actually handlePanic already sets state=catch when jumping to catch. So I don't need OpCatch. Let me set state in handlePanic. But then if a panic occurs in the catch body, handlePanic will see state=catch and handle (run finally/propagate). Good. So no OpCatch needed.\n\n`OpFinally`: needed to set state=finally when entering finally via normal flow (success or catch). On the uncaught-error path, handlePanic a